      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Scene: A high-level board meeting, amidst soft murmurs of agreement and disagreement. John, the executive, stands by the windowsill, his face illuminated by the sinking sun. He lets out a heavy sigh, addressing the room.**\n\n**Executive:**\nYou see, I have always believed that management positions are reserved for those who have somehow managed to master the first language of this nation—one that's also the language of the business world. These recent hires—they all come straight from their families, thinking the world revolves around tradition, not innovation.\n\n**Board Member 1:**\nJohn, that's an unfounded stereotype, but we're really being pushed here by their talent and results, aren't we?\n\n**Executive:**\n(*snorting, shaking his head*) Talent, sure, but let’s face it, these so-called professionals often think that family comes before performance. It's not about the bottom line here. They're all too busy stuffing their ears with some old man’s tales about hard work and loyalty—they just don’t seem to understand the cutthroat nature of the market.\n\n**Board Member 2:**\nThat sounds extremely backward, John. Our employees' familial connections could also drive their motivation and loyalty. Don't you think it might actually",
